 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the air quality in Ipoh is significantly better than in Kuala Lumpur**. \n\nReal-time readings from Malaysia's Department of Environment (APIMS) and international air quality monitors (IQAir / WAQI) show the following:\n\n* **Ipoh (Moderate):** Readings are hovering around **88 to 93 AQI/API** (with stations like Pegoh at ~88 and Tasek at ~90). While there is some light haze, the air quality is considered acceptable for general outdoor activities, though unusually sensitive individuals may want to limit prolonged exertion.\n* **Kuala Lumpur (Unhealthy):** Readings are sitting between **155 and 180 AQI/API** (with Batu Muda at ~160 and Cheras at ~162). Due to ongoing regional haze affecting the Klang Valley and southern Peninsular Malaysia, KL's air is classified as unhealthy for the general public. \n\nIf you are in KL, it is advisable to reduce prolonged outdoor exertion and wear an N95 mask if you need to be outside for extended periods. In Ipoh, conditions are much clearer by comparison.",
